The place I'm starting is with random monster generation.

As turns pass, monsters will start to show up more often, and the
monsters generated will start tending to be of a higher difficulty.
(Extinctionists may actually thank me.)  Dawdle long enough, and the
probability of things showing up that you didn't want to see ("Archon")
starts to be higher and higher.

The slope will have to be rather gradual; you don't want to penalize
players for caution.  But even a cautious player progresses in the game,
so the slope doesn't have to be virtually flat to be effective.  Someone
who takes a long time and while doing so gets themselves to XL30, for 
example, doesn't have much to fear from a few more storm giants than
usual popping in to see.

The variant I'll use on the way back up with the amulet is to tip the
slope up sharply on "frequency of generation"; ie. once you pick up the 
amulet, stuff starts coming out of the walls to stop you.  Most players
are pretty tough at that point, so there's much less need for concern
about "oh noes, they'll run into something unexpected"; by and large,
someone who successfully gets the Amulet has already seen most of the
nasty stuff at least once.  And it should contribute well to a newer
flavor of urgency, _without_ resorting to artificial deus-ex-machina 
restrictions like the mysterious force was.

